<BaseLayout title="BL10 (Tianzhu) — Celestial Pillar Point" description="Everything you need to self-treat BL10 (Tianzhu) — exact location, when to use it, a step-by-step routine, symptom-based point combinations, and expected results." breadcrumbs={[{ label: "Home", href: "/" }, { label: "Points", href: "/points" }, { label: "BL10 (Tianzhu)", href: "/points/bl10-tianzhu-acupressure-point" }]} datePublished="2026-08-19" dateModified="2026-08-19" articleSchema={true}>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 lg:px-6">
    <h1 class="mb-4">BL10 (Tianzhu) — Celestial Pillar</h1>
    <p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 text-lg mb-8">BL10, known as Tianzhu or Celestial Pillar, is a Bladder meridian point on the back of the neck. It is a key point for relaxing upper trapezius tension and is traditionally used for neck pain, stiff neck, headaches, dizziness, and eye strain.</p>

    <section class="mb-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">At a Glance</h2>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 overflow-hidden">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-stone-100 dark:divide-stone-800">
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Meridian</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Bladder (BL)</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Body region</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Back of the neck, just below the base of the skull</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Best for</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Neck pain, stiff neck (wry neck), headaches, dizziness, eye strain</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Difficulty</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Easy to reach yourself with both thumbs</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Hold time</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">30–60 seconds</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Frequency</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">1–3 times daily</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Pairs well with</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300"><a href="/points/gb20-fengchi-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">GB20 (Fengchi)</a>, <a href="/points/gb21-jianjing-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">GB21 (Jianjing)</a>, <a href="/points/li4-hegu-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">LI4 (Hegu)</a></td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Skip if</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Acute neck trauma, or broken skin over the point (see Precautions)</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Why BL10 Matters</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">BL10 sits on the Bladder meridian, which runs down both sides of the spine from the head to the feet. Its location — in the hollow just off the back of the neck, on the lateral edge of the trapezius muscle — places it exactly where upper trapezius tension gathers in people who sit at desks, drive, or hold their heads forward for long periods. That muscle is one of the most common sources of neck pain and tension headaches, which makes BL10 one of the most practical points a person can learn.</p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">Classically, BL10 is used for neck stiffness, stiff neck (the locked neck of a bad night's sleep), headaches, dizziness, and eye problems such as red, swollen, or painful eyes, as well as throat complaints. The Bladder meridian's long course means points at the base of the skull connect local neck tension with head symptoms, so pressing BL10 often brings relief that is felt in the scalp and behind the eyes as well as in the neck itself.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Location</h2>
    <img src="/images/bl10-tianzhu-location.webp" alt="BL10 acupressure point location at the back of the neck" class="w-full max-w-sm mx-auto rounded-xl shadow-sm" width="700" height="620" loading="lazy">
    <p class="mb-3">BL10 lies on the back of the neck, about 0.5 cun above the posterior hairline and 1.3 cun (roughly one and a half finger widths) lateral to the midline, in the depression at the outer border of the trapezius muscle. Slide your thumbs from the top of the spine outward until they land in the hollow just inside the thick band of muscle at the sides of the neck — that is the point.</p>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3"><strong>Three quick confirmations you have the right spot:</strong> (1) the hollow sits just outside the thick band of neck muscle, not on the spine itself; (2) pressing up and inward produces a deep, achy sensation that may spread into the head or behind the eyes; (3) the point is tender in exactly the spot where your neck already feels tight.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">When to Use BL10 (and When Not)</h2>
    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mb-3">
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-emerald-200 bg-emerald-50 dark:border-emerald-900/40 dark:bg-emerald-900/20 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-2 text-sm">Use it when</h3>
        <ul class="space-y-1.5 text-sm text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
          <li>• You feel pain or stiffness at the back of the neck</li>
          <li>• Your neck is locked or painful after sleeping in a bad position</li>
          <li>• A tension headache builds from the base of the skull</li>
          <li>• You feel mild dizziness or eye strain after long screen work</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-stone-50 dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-800/50 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-2 text-sm">Hold off if</h3>
        <ul class="space-y-1.5 text-sm text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
          <li>• You recently had a neck injury, whiplash, or cervical spine fracture</li>
          <li>• There is broken skin, a rash, or swelling on the back of the neck</li>
          <li>• Neck movement triggers dizziness, unsteadiness, or fainting</li>
          <li>• Pressing causes radiating pain, numbness, or tingling into the arm</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">Use the <em>Hold off</em> column as a safety filter: BL10 is a comfort practice for chronic tension, not first aid for an acute neck injury.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Benefits &amp; Uses</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-3 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li><strong>Neck pain:</strong> BL10 helps relieve pain and stiffness in the back of the neck.</li>
      <li><strong>Stiff neck:</strong> It is a traditional point for a locked or "wry" neck after sleeping awkwardly.</li>
      <li><strong>Headache:</strong> BL10 eases tension-type headaches and pain felt at the base of the skull.</li>
      <li><strong>Dizziness:</strong> It is classically used for mild dizziness and vertigo-like sensations.</li>
      <li><strong>Eye strain:</strong> BL10 supports tired, red, or swollen eyes after screen use or long reading.</li>
    </ul>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">How to Massage BL10</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">Use both thumbs at once so the pressure stays even, and press up and inward toward the center of the neck rather than straight back into the spine. Keep the head in neutral so the neck muscles stay relaxed.</p>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li>Sit upright with the head in neutral, jaw relaxed.</li>
      <li>Place both thumbs in the hollows on the outer edges of the trapezius muscles.</li>
      <li>Press up and inward with firm, slow pressure until you feel a deep ache.</li>
      <li>Hold for 30–60 seconds while slowly nodding the head forward and back.</li>
      <li>Release gradually and repeat 1–3 times daily or whenever the neck feels tight.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="mt-3 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed"><strong>Common mistake to avoid:</strong> pressing straight back toward the spine or cranking the pressure up hard, which can trigger dizziness or a radiating nerve pinch. The target sensation is a deep, dull, spreading ache — ease off if it feels sharp, or if you feel lightheaded at all.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Combine BL10 with These Points</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">BL10 works best as part of a neck-and-head sequence. Pick the row closest to your situation and work the points in the order shown, 30–60 seconds each.</p>
    <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 overflow-hidden">
      <table class="w-full text-sm">
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-stone-100 dark:divide-stone-800">
          <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-44">Goal / symptom</th><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">Routine (in order)</th></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Neck pain</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">BL10 → <a href="/points/gb20-fengchi-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">GB20</a> → <a href="/points/gb21-jianjing-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">GB21</a>, 30–60s each, repeat 1–2 times daily</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Stiff neck (wry neck) after sleep</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">BL10 → GB21 → <a href="/points/li4-hegu-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">LI4</a>, then slow, gentle neck movement</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Headache</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">GB20 → BL10 → LI4, holding each 60s for up to 3 minutes total</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Dizziness / eye strain</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">BL10 → GB20 with gentle pressure; avoid sudden head movements during and after</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">For a fuller program, see the <a href="/body/neck-acupressure-points" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">neck acupressure points</a> body guide and our <a href="/conditions/acupressure-for-neck-pain" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">acupressure for neck pain</a> page.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">What to Expect</h2>
    <div class="space-y-3">
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-1 text-sm">First session (today)</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">BL10 is often tender, and pressing it usually brings a deep, releasing sensation within a minute. Neck tightness may ease noticeably right away, though stiffness can return later in the day — treat the first session as a test run.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-1 text-sm">Days 3–7 (consistent daily practice)</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">With 1–2 sessions per day, tenderness at the point usually eases and turning the head often feels smoother. This matches the pattern in studies of acupressure for neck pain and headache, where benefits appear after several days of regular use.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-1 text-sm">Weeks 2–4</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">For chronic neck tension, pair BL10 with posture habits — screen height, chair support, and regular movement breaks. Acupressure is a complement, not a replacement: if neck pain or headaches persist beyond 2–3 weeks despite daily practice, or new symptoms appear, see a clinician.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Science &amp; Research</h2>
    <p class="mb-3">Neck pain and headache are among the conditions most often studied in acupressure research. Systematic reviews and randomized controlled trials have examined acupressure as an adjunct for pain conditions, with results that are generally promising but limited in quality. Meta-analyses of acupressure for pain tend to show a positive direction of effect, though many of the included studies are small and their methodological quality varies considerably.</p>
    <p class="mb-3">Researchers consistently note that acupressure trials are hard to blind, because the treatment is physical and participants can usually tell whether they are being treated. This makes it difficult to separate the specific effects of a particular point from the general benefits of touch, pressure, and relaxation. The overall picture — which we discuss in detail on our <a href="/research/does-acupressure-work">does acupressure work?</a> review — is one of encouraging signals alongside a clear need for larger, higher-quality studies before firm conclusions can be drawn. For neck pain specifically, our <a href="/conditions/acupressure-for-neck-pain" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">acupressure for neck pain</a> page and the <a href="/body/neck-acupressure-points" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">neck acupressure points</a> guide summarize the strongest available evidence.</p>

    <EvidenceBox refs={["hsieh-2010"]} note="The trial above tested acupressure for headache, the neck-and-head complaint this point is most used for. It is not a study of BL10 in isolation." />

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Precautions</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-3 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li>Avoid if you have a recent acute neck injury, whiplash, fracture, or cervical spinal surgery.</li>
      <li>Do not press if there are skin lesions, rashes, or swelling on the back of the neck.</li>
      <li>Avoid this point if you have vertebral artery disease or unexplained dizziness with neck movement.</li>
      <li>Stop if pressure causes dizziness, nausea, or radiating pain into the arm.</li>
    </ul>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Red Flags — See a Professional</h2>
    <div class="rounded-xl border border-red-200 bg-red-50 dark:border-red-900/40 dark:bg-red-900/20 p-4 text-sm">
      <p class="text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300 mb-2">Stop self-treatment and see a doctor or physiotherapist if you notice any of the following:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
        <li>Neck pain that started after a fall, whiplash, or other injury</li>
        <li>Numbness, tingling, or weakness running down the arm or into the hand</li>
        <li>Dizziness, unsteadiness, or fainting with neck movement or pressure</li>
        <li>Severe headache, or neck stiffness with fever</li>
        <li>Pain that has not improved after 2–3 weeks of regular acupressure and gentle movement</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">How do I find BL10?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Place your thumbs on the back of the neck, about one and a half finger widths out from the midline, then slide them down to the hollow just inside the thick band of muscle at the sides of the neck. The point sits roughly half a finger width above the posterior hairline.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Can BL10 help with a stiff neck after a bad night's sleep?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Yes. BL10 is a traditional point for the locked, "wry" neck that follows sleeping awkwardly. Press it gently with both thumbs, combine with GB21 and LI4, and follow with slow, pain-free movement. If the neck stays locked or pain spreads, see a professional.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">How long and how often should I press BL10?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Press for 30–60 seconds with both thumbs, moving the neck slowly while you hold, then ease off gradually. Repeat 1–3 times daily. Stop immediately if you feel dizzy or nauseated, since this area sits close to the brainstem and major vessels.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Can I press BL10 at work?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Yes. BL10 is easy to reach with your own thumbs while seated, making it a natural mid-day break during desk work. A 30–60 second press combined with a shoulder roll and a screen break is enough to reset a tense upper neck.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">When should I stop BL10 and see a doctor?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Stop and see a professional if the neck pain began after an injury, if you have numbness or tingling down the arm, if movement or pressure causes dizziness, or if there has been no improvement after 2–3 weeks of regular acupressure. Severe headache or neck stiffness with fever needs urgent evaluation.</div>
    </details>
    </section>

